00:00:04.310 --> 00:00:09.990 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  to you the viewer this is probably a  standard moment you've seen  and played in dozens of shooters over  the years  but for me this moment was revolutionary  not because of what's happening on  screen but because of what i was feeling  in my hands  thanks to the playstation 5's new dual  sense controller  new consoles tend to set the standard of  gaming for the next five to seven years  better graphics new interfaces and yes  new controllers xbox's new controller is  largely the same with some new ergonomic  designs but sony's really been hyping up  its new  dual sense controller with haptic  feedback and adaptive triggers  something at first i thought would be  another gimmick  like the six axis or touchpad but after  spending a few weeks with the thing it  has become my favorite part of the ps5  rather than demand you play your games  in a new more awkward way  haptic feedback and the adaptive  triggers enhance the way we already play  games  and funny enough it was an astros  playroom the free game that acts as a  demo for the dual sense that sold me on  it  it was this year's call of duty of all  things  because these features are perfect for  shooters  haptic feedback is an evolution of the  standard rumble we've all been using for  the past few decades  previous controllers like the dualshock  4 use motors that create  nice but binary experiences for rumble  it shakes when it's supposed to and  stops when it needs to stop with haptic  feedback it uses voice coil actuators  which  is the same kind of tech that's used to  vibrate speakers  and as a result developers have more  control over how the rumble  operates in the case of call of duty  when i fire this mp5 the dual sense  vibrates to the specific rhythm of the  fire  rate and when you swap to a burst fire  weapon that rhythm changes  the same effect applies to reloading  feeling the controller as you load  each shell is subtle but adds a lot to  the experience  it's the kind of improvement that's hard  to fully express  but you feel the difference right away  in your hand meanwhile there's the  adaptive triggers which takes the idea  of pressure sensitive buttons that the  ps2 had  but this time the controller changes  that pressure based on the current  situation of the  game triggers now have a resistance to  them not unlike an  actual gun and that resistance changes  based on the weapon  pistols can be tapped with your finger  much more quickly while a single shot  rifle requires more  effort to fire it makes every shot feel  more impactful  and there's a real tangible difference  to using each gun  even pulling the left trigger to aim  down sights uses the adaptive triggers  pulling out a machine gun requires a lot  more pressure and tricks your brain into  giving the impression  that this gun is a heavy beast  and when you couple all that with the  haptic feedback and a nice pair of  headphones i have never  felt more immersed shooting a gun with a  standard video game controller  i had a blast playing through cold war's  campaign  and the dual sense is a major reason why  out of curiosity i switched over to the  ps4 version of the game  and it's so hard to go back the old dual  shock  it's fine it served me well for the last  seven years but the dual sense makes it  feel  like a relic of course the option to  turn all these off is  possible on both a game and system level  if you're the kind of person that can't  stand rumble  or you have a disability that prevents  you from using it in fact i can see a  lot of hardcore competitive players  turning the triggers in particular  off in multiplayer you can't really  feather the trigger like you're used to  with the adaptives turned on and it's  easier to snap to players without the  pressure  i won't deny it did feel like i was  performing slightly better without them  leaving me in a situation where i was  choosing between performance  or immersion but that could simply be  because that's  what i'm used to and as i become more  accustomed to the dual sense that  feeling will go away  i predict a lot of debate around this in  pvp communities in the coming months  but for me as somebody who primarily  enjoys single player experiences  i'm in love with the dual sense and  what's most exciting is that  this is just the first attempt as more  developers get their hands on this the  implementation will only improve  you hear examples from developers like  the upcoming death loop who talk about  how the triggers will lock up when the  gun jams  and my mind races with the possibilities  just imagine what a developer like hideo  kojima can do with this  but that does bring up the concern that  devs do need to put in the time to  properly implement it call of duty has a  deal with sony that pushes the marketing  for the game on the playstation  so it makes sense that they went all out  with it meanwhile assassin's creed  valhalla was marketed for the  xbox and it doesn't do anything special  with the haptic feedback  and doesn't even use the adaptive  triggers at all spending extra time and  money on features that will only apply  to one system  might not always be possible but i am  hopeful that this won't go the same  route as six axis did back on the ps3  it's a far more subtle and useful  feature that can be applied to games  without needing to be built around it  at the very least i think i'm going to  be buying shooters on the ps5  for the foreseeable future  oh

